    "created_at": "2023-10-10T00:44:49Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-10-10T00:49:02Z",
    "body": "> in implementing a regular expression engine in a safe language.\r\n\r\nYou mean in an *unsafe* language right?\r\n\r\n> Any untrusted input at all falls afoul of [the rule of 2](https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/master/docs/security/rule-of-2.md), at least in Chromium.)\r\n\r\nThe article you've linked mentions that Chromium does have [a trusted regex library, RE2,](https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/master/docs/security/rule-of-2.md#normalization). Would it be viable in chromium to limit regexpes in URLPatterns to *some* common subset that is shared with RE2? From [the RE2 docs](https://github.com/google/re2/wiki/Syntax) it does seem like a fairly large subset should be viable.\r\n\r\n@annevk  I presume Firefox doesn't have this limitation as any regex engine for this purpose could just be written in Rust right?",
